<!--
  Homo-oligomer design: RFdiffusion builds a C3 symmetric backbone from
  nothing and LigandMPNN gives its three chains one shared sequence.

      rosetta_link_scripts \
          -parser:protocol examples/homo_oligomer_design.xml \
          -out:path output/homo_oligomer

  No input structure: the contig names only de novo residues, so there is no
  -s and no inference.input_pdb. 2 backbones x 2 sequences = 4 designs.

  RFdiffusion samples symmetric assemblies from a config of its own, named in
  extra_args. Under it the contig is one length, the total over every chain,
  and it has to divide evenly by the number of chains: 180 is 3 x 60. Written
  as a range, because a bare number reaches RFdiffusion as an integer and it
  reads its contigs as strings.

  The override comes before the config-name flag on purpose. RFdiffusion
  reads its overrides as positional arguments and stops collecting them at the
  first flag, so anything written after that flag is rejected as an
  unrecognised argument.

  TiedPositions ties the three chains position by position, so each group
  holds one residue per chain and the three chains come out identical.

  LigandMPNN also has a homo_oligomer option that builds the same groups
  itself, and it cannot be used here. It takes the residue numbers of one
  chain and puts every chain letter in front of them, which needs each chain
  to repeat the same numbering. RFdiffusion numbers a symmetric design right
  through: chain A is 1-60, chain B is 61-120, chain C is 121-180, so that
  option looks for a residue B121 which does not exist and LigandMPNN stops
  with a KeyError. TiedPositions reads the residues from the pose instead, so
  it ties A1, B61 and C121. Use homo_oligomer on a structure whose chains each
  start at 1, as a deposited trimer does.

  scRMSD_all pairs residue i of chain A with residue i of chain A, chain B
  with chain B and so on, so it says something only when the prediction names
  the chains the way the design does. A predictor has no reason to. On a
  homotrimer it can label them in the opposite cyclic order, which no rotation
  undoes, and the number then comes out large for a structure that matches.
  scRMSD_chain covers one chain against that same chain and is free of it.
  Read it for self consistency, and treat a large scRMSD_all on a
  homo-oligomer as a question rather than an answer.

  The design and prediction stages are in a FOR_EACH_POSE because
  TiedPositions takes a single input pose: symmetry_residues has no _multi
  counterpart, so one list covers a whole LigandMPNN run and the two backbones
  have to be designed one at a time.
-->
<ROSETTASCRIPTS>

    <RESIDUE_SELECTORS>
        <Chain name="chain_A" chains="A" />
        <Chain name="chain_B" chains="B" />
        <Chain name="chain_C" chains="C" />
    </RESIDUE_SELECTORS>

    <MOVERS>
        <RFdiffusion name="design_backbone"
            contig="[180-180]"
            num_designs="2"
            extra_args="inference.symmetry=c3 --config-name symmetry"
            prefix_name="rfd_"
            work_dir="01_rfdiff" />

        <LigandMPNN name="design_sequence"
            batch_size="2"
            temperature="0.3"
            prefix_name="mpnn_"
            work_dir="02_ligandmpnn">
            <TiedPositions residues_selectors="chain_A,chain_B,chain_C" />
        </LigandMPNN>

        <ColabFold name="predict_structure"
            models="1,2"
            msa_mode="single_sequence"
            prefix_name="AF2_"
            replace_pose="true"
            work_dir="03_colabfold">
            <RMSD name="scRMSD_all" />
            <RMSD name="scRMSD_chain" calculation_selector="chain_A" />
            <metric name="plddt_all" base_metric="pLDDT" />
            <metric name="pAE_all" base_metric="pAE" />
        </ColabFold>
    </MOVERS>

    <PROTOCOLS>
        <Add mover="design_backbone" />
        <FOR_EACH_POSE><PROTOCOLS>
            <Add mover="design_sequence" />
            <Add mover="predict_structure" />
        </PROTOCOLS></FOR_EACH_POSE>
    </PROTOCOLS>

</ROSETTASCRIPTS>
